   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to child advocacy centers",
    "basePrintNo": "S8677",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8677-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §424-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8677\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to child advocacy\ncenters\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide all qualified child advocacy\ncenters with equal access to information contained in the statewide\ncentral register of child abuse and maltreatment.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subsection 3 of section 424-a of the social services\nlaw to include private, nonprofit child advocacy centers in the defi-\nnition of \"provider\" or \"provider agency\". Section 2 establishes the\neffective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChild advocacy centers must comply with standards set by the Office of\nChildren and Family Services (OCFS) and exist to respond to cases\ninvolving child victims of sexual abuse and serious physical abuse.\nThese child advocacy centers provide a coordinated and cooperative\nresponse in an expert and timely manner that minimizes trauma to youth-\nful victims and their non-offending family members. To provide their\nservices, centers commonly rely on employment, contractual, and/or\nvolunteer arrangements with physicians and medical providers trained in\nforensic pediatrics, mental health professionals, and victim advocacy\npersonnel. Because these individuals have regular and substantial\ncontact with children, it is essential that centers have the capability\nto vet prospective and current personnel through a comprehensive back-\nground check, including inquiries to the State Central Register of Child\nAbuse and Maltreatment (SCR). New York state does not currently grant\nequal access to the SCR to all child advocacy centers. Centers which are\ncounty-run have authority to access the SCR as \"provider agencies\" to\nvet current and future personnel, but private, not-for-profit child\nadvocacy centers do not. The discrepancy derives from a failure to cate-\ngorically identify \"child advocacy centers\" as \"provider agencies.\" By\nremedying the disparate treatment of county-run and private child advo-\ncacy centers, all centers will have the same authority to vet prospec-\ntive and current personnel to enable them to protect the children they\nserve.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
